one for each brand.



Prompt:

【MAIN WORD】: {4–6 letter uppercase keyword}
【SUBJECT】: {person / product / scene}
【CAMPAIGN TYPE】: {beverage / sports / food / lifestyle}
【DYNAMIC ELEMENT】: {liquid / bubbles / noodles / steam / dust / motion trail}
【BRAND / PRODUCT】: {name}
【BACKGROUND】: {color}
【PALETTE】: {main colors}
【TOP LEFT】: {date / drop / venue}
【TOP RIGHT】: {3 short concept words}
【BOTTOM LEFT】: {materials / features / details}
【BOTTOM RIGHT】: {brand / product name}
【FORMAT】: 9:16 vertical

Create a high-end campaign poster using vertical sliced typography.

Place 【MAIN WORD】 large at the center, occupying about 80–88% of the width. Keep it bold, clear and readable at thumbnail size.

Use the entire word as a photographic mask. Embed one single continuous commercial photograph featuring 【SUBJECT】 and 【DYNAMIC ELEMENT】 across all letters.

Build the image around one clear subject, one strong visual anchor, and one dynamic element that carries motion through the word. The scene should feel alive, but still clean and controlled.

Keep roughly the upper 45–50% of the letterforms readable. Extend selected lower strokes downward into 4–6 vertical sections with varied lengths, using them for secondary details such as liquid, steam, texture, reflections, shadows or environment.

The same photograph must continue naturally through every letter and slice. Motion lines, product shapes, limbs, tools, food, or dynamic elements should align correctly across the word.

Strict clipping rule: all people, products, props, liquid, steam, dust and other photographic elements must stay completely inside the typography. Nothing should cross into the outer negative space.

Use a clean 【BACKGROUND】 with generous negative space and 【PALETTE】 for the photography. Add a subtle soft shadow behind the typography, slightly offset to the lower right.

Arrange supporting information around the composition:

Top left: 【TOP LEFT】
Top right: 【TOP RIGHT】
Bottom left: 【BOTTOM LEFT】
Bottom right: 【BOTTOM RIGHT】

Use small, precise modern sans-serif typography.

The final result should feel like a finished commercial campaign or editorial poster, not a template demo.

Avoid different images inside each letter, broken subjects, excessive slices, barcode-like strips, elements crossing outside the type mask, random placeholder text, heavy 3D effects, watermarks, numbering, Vol., No., sparkle symbols or four-point stars.
